\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Configuration Clifford algebras}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{How might a discrete first-order equation acquire the internal
components used to describe matter with spin? For two commuting centered
differences, try #{D=a\nabla_1+b\nabla_2}. Relations
#{a^2=b^2=I} and #{ab+ba=0} cancel the mixed term in its square.
Real or complex scalars cannot satisfy them, but the
[two-by-two matrices #{X,Z}](fdip-000R) do. This is an algebraic
two-component example, not a derivation of electron spin. With these
self-adjoint matrices and skew-adjoint centered differences,
the positive square is #{-D^2};
it uses two-step shifts and [differs from the nearest-neighbor
Laplacian](fdip-000T).}

\p{The common algebraic question is how a quadratic form can be
encoded by products of generators. Here the
[configuration quadratic form](fdip-000O) determines a
[Clifford algebra](fdip-000P) through its square relation.
Keeping the polar normalization explicit lets
[form-preserving maps](fdip-000Q) act functorially, without identifying
a quadratic map with a chosen coordinate array. This algebra encodes
the supplied configuration form; it does not choose the spatial
Clifford module used for [Dirac operators](fdip-001C).}

\transclude{fdip-000O}
\transclude{fdip-000P}
\transclude{fdip-000Q}
